<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>G/Ker(φ) ≅ Im(φ)</title><link href="https://gmodk.github.io/" rel="alternate"/><link href="https://gmodk.github.io/feeds/all-en.atom.xml" rel="self"/><id>https://gmodk.github.io/</id><updated>2026-10-11T00:00:00-03:00</updated><subtitle>Explorations of a curious mind on mathematical structures and abstraction.</subtitle><entry><title>program_execution_individual::(2/2)</title><link href="https://gmodk.github.io/program-execution-individual-2-2/" rel="alternate"/><published>2026-10-11T00:00:00-03:00</published><updated>2026-10-11T00:00:00-03:00</updated><author><name>gmodk</name></author><id>tag:gmodk.github.io,2026-10-11:/program-execution-individual-2-2/</id><summary type="html"/><content type="html">&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;... &lt;a href="https://gmodk.github.io/program-execution-individual-1-2/"&gt;program_execution_individual::(1/2)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If a system develops from a set of initial specifications, what structures could distinguish between
predeterminated information and genuinely path-dependent organization?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What invariants would have to persist through development for we to say that one
evolving system remains numerically the same individual?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;a) Set up&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Let the function &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(\phi: P x C \to R\)&lt;/span&gt; to map a tuple &lt;span class="math"&gt;\((p,c)\)&lt;/span&gt;  to a realization &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(r\)&lt;/span&gt;, where &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(p \in P\)&lt;/span&gt; is a program and &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(c \in C\)&lt;/span&gt; is a contingency (history).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Let the map &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(H_{\alpha}: R \to D_{\alpha}\)&lt;/span&gt; to extract the organization into some descriptive level. Then, we have:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="math"&gt;$$
    H_{\alpha}(P,C) = H_{\alpha}(\phi(p,c))
$$&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;which is the realized organization along a particular path.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;b) Formally&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The path-dependent part is the variation of the &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(H_{\alpha}\)&lt;/span&gt; over &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(C\)&lt;/span&gt;. If &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(C\)&lt;/span&gt; has a probability measure &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(\mu\)&lt;/span&gt;, we define its predictable component by:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="math"&gt;$$
    \bar{H}_{\alpha}(p) = \int_{c}^{\infty} H_{\alpha}(p,c) \, d{\mu(c)} = E[H_{\alpha} | P = p]
$$&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And the path-dependent derivation by:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="math"&gt;$$
    \Delta_{\alpha}(p,c) = H_{\alpha} - \bar{H}_{\alpha}(p-c)
$$&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;c) Conclusion&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We conclude, therefore that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="math"&gt;$$
    H_{\alpha}(p,c) = H_{\alpha}(p) + \Delta_{\alpha}(p,c)
$$&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;where:
- &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(H_{\alpha}(p)\)&lt;/span&gt; is the predictable path from &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(p\)&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span class="math"&gt;\(\Delta_{\alpha}(p,c)\)&lt;/span&gt; is the history dependant path.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But the integral represents an expected or predictable component, not necessarily strcited predetermined information, which would required invariance across all &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(c \in C\)&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;d) Intuitevely&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The program defines what can systematically be expected across all possible histories, while (&lt;span class="math"&gt;\(H_{\alpha}(p) \circ \phi\)&lt;/span&gt;) captures what actually emerges in one particular history.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Averaging over &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(C\)&lt;/span&gt; extracts the common predictable structure, and the contribution of the specific path to the representation is recored by the resisudal.&lt;/p&gt;
