&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What mathematical distinctions would we need to separate a Program, its Execution,
and an emergent Individual without assuming in advance that these are three different
ontological kinds?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;a) Set up&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Let &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(P\)&lt;/span&gt; be a genrative structure, &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(C\)&lt;/span&gt; the space of initial conditions or contingences,
and &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(I\)&lt;/span&gt; the space of possible realizations. We define a map &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(\phi: P x C \to I\)&lt;/span&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Supose &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(R = Im(\phi)\)&lt;/span&gt; is the set of realizable executions. A level of description is then
given by a structure preserving map:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="math"&gt;$$
    H_{\alpha}: R \to D_{\alpha}
$$&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;with &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(R = Im(\phi) \subseteq I\)&lt;/span&gt; and &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(\phi(P,C) = i\)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;b) Formally
For each descriptive map &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(H_{\alpha}\)&lt;/span&gt;, we define:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="math"&gt;$$
    r_1 ~ r_2 &amp;lt;=&amp;gt; H_{\alpha}(r_1) = H_{\alpha}(r_2)
$$&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Then, the descriptive level is the quotient:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="math"&gt;$$
    D_{\alpha} \cong \frac{R}{r_{\alpha}}
$$&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is equivalent to:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="math"&gt;$$
    \frac{R}{Ker H_{\alpha}} \cong Im H_{\alpha}
$$&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;c) Conclusion&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The program, its execution and the emergent individual need not to be three different ontological kinds. They can be understood as different mathematical roles within one structure, where:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;P specifies possibilites.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;R contains realized trajectories.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An individual can be represented as an equivalence class &lt;span class="math"&gt;\([r_{\alpha}]\)&lt;/span&gt;
at a chosen description level &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(D_{\alpha}\)&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;d) Intuitevely&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The idea is that a system contains more detail that any description of that system prserveres. The map &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(\phi\)&lt;/span&gt; generates actual realizations from the program, while each &lt;span class="math"&gt;\(H_{\alpha}\)&lt;/span&gt;
forgets some distinctions and keeps others.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Two different realizations may tehrefore count as the same individual whenerver they
become identical under the chosen description, so individuality emerges from the structure
we preserve across transformations rather than being assumed beforehand.&lt;/p&gt;
